      "obligation": "refuter:2:1",
      "id": "thm-stationary-splitting-converges-iff-the-iteration-matrix-has-spectral-radius-below-one",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The cited definition permits arbitrary fields: “Let $A\\in M_n(\\mathbb F)$ admit a matrix splitting” with $M\\in\\operatorname{GL}_n(\\mathbb F)$. This Statement only says “Let $A=M-N$ be a matrix splitting” but then asserts convergence and $\\rho(B)<1$. For, e.g., $\\mathbb F=\\mathbb F_2$, neither a spectral radius ordered against $1$ nor topological convergence is defined; moreover the proof's “after extending scalars to $\\mathbb C$” is impossible across characteristic. The theorem needs a real or complex scalar-field hypothesis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "087413d174bb8001bd86a87d5fc761e16981fd507de08ca234cf2b7e93759bf3"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:2",
      "id": "thm-conjugate-gradient-terminates-by-the-relative-grade-in-exact-arithmetic",
      "location": "Proof 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says “Apply [L2] with $m=\\nu$” and concludes “the unique minimizer is $x_\\nu=x_*$.” But [L2] applies only after introducing “the $m$th CG iterate,” while the recurrence defines an update only “for every index $k$ with $r_k\\ne0$.” If CG has already reached $x_*$ at an earlier step, $x_\\nu$ is not defined by that recurrence. A brief separate early-termination case would close the gap.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
